CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=2c918084-5e4b21a8-015e-58b5eff5-00001139:Meyerhoff\
 , Eli
CREATED:20260316T171651Z
DESCRIPTION:The workshop\, "Varieties of Harmony in Greek and Chinese Phil
 osophy\," will take place at Duke University on April 16-17\, 2026\, with
  sessions running 9:00 AM-5:00 PM each day. It is an interdisciplinary wo
 rkshop that brings together scholars of ancient Greek philosophy and earl
 y Chinese philosophy to explore different conceptions of interpersonal ha
 rmony\, including family relationships\, friendship\, civic cooperation\,
  ethical cultivation\, and social/political relations. The event aims to 
 foster comparative dialogue across traditions and to highlight both conve
 rgences and important differences in how interpersonal harmony is underst
 ood and cultivated. The workshop is organized by Wenjin Liu and David Won
 g.\n\nDate: April 16-17\, 9am-5pm\n\nLocation: Smith Warehouse\, Bay 4\, 
 Room C105\, Ahmadieh Lecture Hall\n\nVirtual (Zoom) option also available
  - please register for in-person attendance or virtual attendance below.\
 n\nSpeakers include:\n\nDimitri El Murr (École Normale Supérieure)\nEmily
  Fletcher (University of Wisconsin - Madison)\nChris Fraser (Chinese Univ
 ersity of Hong Kong)\nRachana Kamtekar (Cornell University)\nJing Hu (Con
 cordia University)\nMitzi Lee (Colorado University - Boulder)\nWenjin Liu
  (Duke University)\nJames Miller (Duke Kunshan University)\nJustin Tiwald
  (University of Hong Kong)\nHwa Yeong Wang (Duke Kunshan University)\nDav
 id Wong (Duke University)\n\nClick here to register for in-person attenda
 nce at the conference: https://forms.gle/UA2Msw9HAyUnKm5q7\n\nTo register
  for attendance virtually via Zoom\, click here: https://duke.zoom.us/mee
 ting/register/tsqfEdJkSHyCO99B7LzRSQ\n\nSee the full program here: https:
 //sites.duke.edu/harmony/varieties-of-harmony-in-greek-and-chinese-philos
 ophy/\n\nCo-sponsors: Harmony Humanities Lab at the John Hope Franklin Hu
 manities Institute\, Duke Center for Comparative Philosophy\, Duke Depart
 ment of Philosophy\, Duke Kunshan University's Humanities Research Center
 
DURATION:PT8H
DTSTAMP:20260410T181920Z
